{
  "is_sufficient": false,
  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "The perturbations are underspecified: user and candidate counts, matching tolerances, timestamp handling, shuffle sampling, tie-breaking, bootstrap settings, and runtime limits are missing.",
    "Deleting events changes both sequence content and positional indices. The report needs controls separating these effects, plus a precise availability-constrained shuffle algorithm and reporting of unchanged or infeasible shuffles.",
    "The adapter measures sensitivity but provides no explicit rule connecting findings to model changes or validation improvements. Its future-potential rating of 8 is speculative, and implementation difficulty may be understated.",
    "Repeated validation-based search can overfit the fixed 800-user validation sample. Diagnostic sampling and execution also need explicit bounds within the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Establish a reproducible shuffle procedure and determine whether availability constraints leave enough valid permutations to measure order sensitivity.",
      "query": "How does 'Disentangling User and Item Sequence Patterns' (RecSys 2025) implement availability-constrained history shuffling? Examine permutation sampling, timestamp ties, first-observed item availability, unchanged histories, and feasible-permutation coverage when adapting the method to left-padded SASRec sequences using training interactions only."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Separate event-content effects from positional changes and replace vague matching instructions with implementable controls.",
      "query": "What controlled history-perturbation methods for SASRec distinguish recent-item dependence from positional-embedding and sequence-length effects? Compare deletion with compaction, position-preserving masking, recent-event removal matched to older events by training popularity and title similarity, and fixed-candidate ranking comparisons; identify matching tolerances, missing-match handling, and rank-displacement definitions."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Determine whether the diagnostic extension can guide useful model changes without excessive computation or adaptation to the fixed validation sample.",
      "query": "What evidence shows that sequential-recommender history-perturbation diagnostics predict useful architecture or training ablations? Investigate prespecified diagnostic-to-ablation rules, paired validation comparisons, and repeated-selection bias for SASRec under a fixed four-dataset macro HR@10 benchmark with 200 validation users per dataset, 99 negatives per user, and a shared 12 GPU-hour search budget."
    }
  ]
}
